               ]
            ],
            [
               "Power",
               5,
               "x"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "\\( 5^{x} \\) is an exponential function with a constant base, so its derivative is \\( 5^{x} \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)} \\); the power rule does not apply to it.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Power",
               5,
               "x"
            ],
            [
               "Ln",
               5
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Write the root as the power \\( \\left(1 + \\sin(4x)\\right)^{1/2} \\) so the power rule applies to the outer layer. The factor \\( \\sqrt{1 + \\sin(4x)} \\) is a square root of \\( 1 + \\sin(4x) \\), which contains a sine of \\( 4x \\), so apply the chain rule from the outside inward: \\( \\left(\\sqrt{1 + \\sin(4x)}\\right)' = \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\sqrt{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 1}} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
